  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 157 PM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nYou're going to be shocked at this, but Tuesday is also looking dry \nand hot with southwesterly flow. Confidence in the heat is still \npretty good with interquartile spreads of about 5 degrees (mid 90s \nto 100 degrees).\n\nShortwave energy will be riding over the ridge Tuesday night into \nWednesday, which could bring some isolated showers in south-central \nMissouri throughout the day. Despite the introduction of a little \ncloud cover, Wednesday looks to be the hottest day this week. \nSouthwesterly flow will be perfectly poised to advect in the warmest \nairmass possible. Highs will soar to near 100 for most, with only \nthose in the lower elevations out east remaining in the mid 90s. \nHeat Index values will approach 110 for much of the area.\n\nThe ridge appears to start breaking down Wednesday night. Global \nmodels show more shortwave energy moving into the region and surface \nlow pressure centers/fronts beginning to take over, at least across \nnorthern and central Missouri. As it stands, most of the \nprecipitation potential remains north of our area. Much like last \nweek, the surface front looks to stall to our north rather than \ntruly pass through, allowing hot conditions to persist particularly \nfor the south and west. There will definitely be a noticeable \ncooldown Thursday through late week for those generally west of 65, \nbut Heat Index values will still be near 100. Locations along the \nsouthern and western Missouri borders in particular may need heat \nheadline extensions beyond Wednesday.\n\nThe CPC still expects Extreme Heat risk headed into the 8-14 day \nperiod, so summer is definitely still in full swing.",
